  "query_preview": "..Yesterday was an active monsoon day across \nSoutheastern Arizona with recorded wind gusts of 58-88 mph, with\na 74 mph wind gust recorded at Tucson International Airport. \nAlong with the strong outflow winds, there was dust storms \nbringing near zero visibilities. \n\nA weak outflow from mexico is currently triggering\nweak showers/thunderstorms for areas south of Tucson in southern\nCochise and Santa Cruz counties. With this lingering overnight activity\nand the needed time for the atmosphere to recover, leading to a\nlater start time for storm initiation this afternoon/evening.\nThis is showing up with the CAMs having initiation in the White\nMountains around noon/1 PM then more widespread initiation 2 to\n3 PM. Storms will begin in the NE then slowly move SW/W over the\ncourse of the afternoon and evening. Tucson will begin to have\nstorm chances beginning after 5/6 PM and moving westward into\nthe evening. Main impacts for storms today follow the\nwind/blowing dust threat area wide, then an added flash flooding\nthreat areas south of Tucson along the International Border in\nsouthern Cochise and Santa Cruz Counties. The 04/00Z HREF once\nagain is showing a large area of 10% probability of 50 kts (and\na small 30% probability as well). With this, the threat for\nblowing dust continues with the storm outflows, so a Blowing\nDust has been issued for the portions of Graham and Cochise\ncounties. As for the flash flooding component, the 04/00Z HREF \nis once again highlighting Cochise and Santa Cruz counties with\nthe 3 hour probability matched mean introducing a 10%\nprobability for 3 inches of accumulation in these areas. \n\nWednesday looks like a downtick in coverage and intensity, \nhowever the moisture and favorable flow continues, so chances \nfor afternoon and evening thunderstorms remain.\n\nFavorable flow for monsoon activity continues through the week \nwith the upper high drifting north towards the Four Corners with\nday to day variability. Forecast high temperatures will fall to\n2-4 degrees above normal, with some variability due to \nthunderstorm activity and cloud cover.",
